# Ubuntu .deb dependencies for //third_party/libpcap, twelve rules: six amd64 and six arm64.
#
# EVERY ONE CARRIES TWO URLS ON PURPOSE. Bazel tries them in order and every rule pins a
# sha256, so a mirror is verified exactly like the origin -- there is no trust cost to listing
# one, only a resilience gain.
#
# The amd64 six are mirrored because archive.ubuntu.com is unreachable, full stop -- not just
# from CI. Six simultaneous "Connect timed out" failures took down the loading phase and, with
# it, //third_party/libpcap and everything depending on it:
#
#   ERROR: loading failure: ... Error downloading
#   [http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/pool/main/libp/libpcap/libpcap0.8-dev_...deb]: Connect timed out
#   ERROR: //third_party/libpcap:pcap: analysis failure
#
# It is NOT a port-80 block, which was the first guess: the arm64 six are also plain HTTP, on
# ports.ubuntu.com, and none failed in the same run. The host itself is the problem.
# archive.ubuntu.com is round-robin DNS across a mirror set, serves no HTTPS at all (curl
# returns 000, so switching scheme in place is not an option), and currently times out from
# developer machines too.
#
# WHY THIS LOOKED CI-SPECIFIC AND WAS NOT. A local `bazel build --config=ci //...` succeeds
# while CI fails, which reads like an environment difference. It is a warm repository cache:
# //.bazelrc sets --repository_cache=third_party/bazel-repo-cache, these files landed there
# during an older fetch, and nothing has needed to reach the network for them since. The cache
# is content-addressed -- look for content_addressable/sha256/<sha>/file, not for *.deb -- so
# it is easy to conclude the files are absent when they are not.
#
# So the mirror is not a CI workaround. It repairs a single point of failure that any cold
# checkout hits: a new machine, a fresh clone, a pruned cache. mirrors.edge.kernel.org carries
# the same pool over HTTPS and answers in ~0.5s.
#
# The arm64 six keep ports.ubuntu.com but lead with its HTTPS endpoint, which does answer.
# They have not failed, so this is prophylactic rather than a fix.
#
# distdir is NOT the answer here. //third_party/distdir is gitignored and documented as a
# cache rather than vendored source, restored in Forgejo through actions/cache -- a mechanism
# BuildBuddy Workflows has no equivalent of, so a fresh runner starts empty regardless.
